{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:3cc773dfa39ee34d4debbc64acb679eb49966c4626d095088cf2b15f04c08c18","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.13,"B":0.5,"C":0.22,"D":0.15}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: Historian, 19/44/17/20. Applied the lexical-priority lock cell by cell against MY OWN CQ3 (P10 0.85 / P50 1.85 / P90 2.75, so sigma_left 0.78 and sigma_right 0.68) and my own OECD-38 core inflation distribution for the 2032-36 SUB-window. INFLATION OBJECT, derived from my CQ2 rather than guessed separately so the two answers are one object: my CQ2 conjunct-A marginal of 0.48 is built on a front-loaded path (2027-29 at ~2.8% on the observed 3.1% core PCE and a tightening Fed) with a decade average near 2.45%, which implies a 2030-36 stretch of (2.45 - 0.84)/0.7 = 2.30%; I carry 2.35% for 2032-36 to reflect the extra persistence the E1 print buys, with sigma 0.55 (wider than CQ2's 10-yr 0.45 because a 5-yr sub-window averages fewer partly-independent sub-periods). P(core >= 2.7) = P(z >= 0.636) = 0.262; P(core < 2.0) = 0.262. STEP 1, A if growth >= 2.6: P(z >= (2.6-1.85)/0.68) = P(z >= 1.103) = 0.135 -> A = 13. STEP 2, else C if core >= 2.7 AND growth < 2.0: P(growth < 2.0) = P(z <= 0.22) = 0.587; stagflation couples them positively, rho = +0.30; joint = 0.262(0.587) + 0.30xsqrt(0.262x0.738x0.587x0.413) = 0.1538 + 0.30(0.2165) = 0.2188 -> C = 22. Disjoint from A since growth < 2.0 lies strictly inside growth < 2.6. STEP 3, else D if growth < 1.7 AND core < 2.0: P(growth < 1.7) = P(z <= -0.192) = 0.424 on the left sigma; rho = +0.18 (the BIS Box C demand-deficit mechanism couples them positively, the supply-wall mechanism negatively, so I sit between Fermi's 0.10 and the Historian's implied ~0.60-conditional); joint = 0.424(0.262) + 0.18(0.2173) = 0.1502 -> D = 15. Disjoint from C on inflation and from A on growth. STEP 4, B = residual = 100 - 13.5 - 21.9 - 15.0 = 49.6 -> 50. SUM = 13 + 50 + 22 + 15 = 100 exactly. EXPLICIT CONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ3: my P50 is 1.85%, so the lock forbids most mass on A and I have not placed it there — A = 13 is exactly P(growth >= 2.6) recomputable from my stated P90 of 2.75 with sigma_right 0.68, and a reader who redoes that arithmetic must get 13-14. My P50 sits inside B's literal 1.7-2.5% band and B is my modal regime. EXPLICIT CONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ2: CQ2's first conjunct puts P(2027-36 core >= 2.50%) at 0.48; CQ6-C needs the strictly tighter condition of 2032-36 core >= 2.70%, which I put at 0.262 — lower, as it must be, and lower by about the right amount given a +20bp higher threshold and a -10bp lower sub-window central. C at 22 then sits below 26.2 because of the added growth condition, and my CQ2 joint of 27 is below 48 because it additionally conditions on China at 0.60. All three are subsets or siblings of one inflation object and none contradicts another. RESIDUAL-CELL AUDIT: B's 50 is ~37pp of mass in growth 1.7-2.6 plus ~13pp from the cells the lock dumps into B (growth < 1.7 with core 2.0-2.7, and growth 2.0-2.6 with core >= 2.7). DIRECTION, per cell, against the Historian's 19/44/17/20: A MODERATED down 6 (the China-leg structural correction to CQ3 plus the half-weighted real-yield signal), C AMPLIFIED up 5 and D MODERATED down 5 (both driven by the same inflation telemetry — core PCE 3.1% rising with a hiking Fed, and China exiting deflation rather than exporting it — which moves mass from the disinflationary-stagnation cell into the stagflationary-squeeze cell), B AMPLIFIED up 6 as the residual. SUBSTANTIVE READ, and it is not a hedge toward the centre: a real term premium of +1.15% with a 42%-probable repression wedge IS the barbell, because it rewards incumbents financing